SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING - THE WEEKEND READ
Saturday, February 28, 2026

In 1961 the Grocery Manufacturers Association settled on a 48-by-40-inch wooden pallet, mostly because that was the size that fit two-across in a boxcar door. Sixty-five years later, rack spacing, dock heights, forklift mast widths, trailer interiors and the footprint of every automated storage system sold in North America are all descended from that decision.

This matters for the current wave of warehouse automation more than the vendors like to admit. A robot that handles cases beautifully but expects a European 1200-by-800 footprint will spend its life in re-palletizing cells at the dock. The most successful automation deployments we have visited did not start with the robot; they started with a floor survey of what actually arrives, on what, in what condition. About a fifth of inbound pallets at a typical food distributor are damaged, mixed, or the wrong size, and no amount of machine vision fixes a splintered stringer.

There is a second lesson. Standards that look arbitrary are load-bearing. The 48-by-40 pallet is also why a 53-foot trailer holds 26 pallets and not 30, why "pallet positions" is the unit in every 3PL contract, and why the plastic pallet pools that promised to replace wood in the 2000s never got past 10% share: you cannot change one dimension of a system that has spent six decades optimizing around it.

If you are selling software into plants and warehouses, the analogous standard is often a decades-old spreadsheet. Treat it with the same respect.

